-- title:
--   Frobenius twist of the H₁ branch after place extension
-- statement:
--   The setting is the following. A prime $q$ and a natural number $M'$ with $M' \neq 0$ are given, with $q \nmid M'$; a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$ is fixed.
--
--   **The valuation-theoretic frame over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ lies in the nonunits of $A$; $W$ is a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over the residue field of $A$, and `hW` says that $W$ is exactly the set `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)` of supersingular places (rational, affine-geometric, with value of the geometric $j$-generator in the supersingular $j$-set); `hle` asserts the inclusion `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'` of the base-changed full modular function field of level $M'$ in the base-changed $X_H$-field for $H =$ `levelH q M'` at level $q^2M'$. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` — a valuation subring of integers, a surjective residue map with kernel the maximal ideal, compatible with $A$ on constants, together with a map on places preserving degrees and compatible with orders — and `hR₀` requires that this residue map compute coefficientwise reduction: for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose image under `coeffMap A.subtype` lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that element lies in $R_0$'s integers and its residue, viewed as a Laurent series over the residue field of $A$, equals `coeffMap (residue A) y`. Finally $s$ is an element of $W$.
--
--   **Small constants $k_0$.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lies in $A$; writing $A_0 = A \cap k_0$ for the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` state that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, henselian, with algebraically closed residue field, and `hκ` states that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   **Roots of unity and the level structure.** $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell_g)$-th root of unity, and `hιξ` posits an embedding $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell_g))$. $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times}$ equal to `levelH q M'` (the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$-level given by `ZMod.unitsMap (dvd_sq_mul q M')`) intersected with the kernel of reduction modulo $\ell_g$. $K$ is the intermediate field of $k_0 \subseteq$ `LaurentSeries k₀` equal to the base change to $k_0$ of the function field `xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`; $K$ carries an $A_0$-algebra structure compatible with $k_0$, and $j \in K$ is the element whose Laurent series is the coefficient image of the classical $q$-expansion `jq`, assumed non-zero.
--
--   **Functoriality of the rigid $H_1$ datum.** The hypotheses `hℓ`, `hM`, `hL` are the three covariance conditions needed to assemble [`WeierstrassCurve.DrinfeldGlobal.rigidDataH1Pow`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassH1Pow.html#L65): that a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point (an affine equation, vanishing of `preΨ ℓg` at $x_P$, and $Q = P$) transports along a variable change; that `IsGamma0PowAt` for $(p,k)$ transports along a variable change via `kernelVariableChangeDeg` in degree `gamma0PowDeg p k`; and that divisibility of `inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x` transports likewise. $\mathcal{G}$ is a family of relative group laws on the projective models over $A_0$-algebras, assumed chord-and-tangent (`h𝒢`) and with origin the identity section (`h𝒢O`), and $\mathcal{T}$ is a `LevelTransport` for $\mathcal{G}$ at $q$, assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` provide, for each $A_0$-algebra and each variable change, respectively each $A_0$-algebra map, a graded ring homomorphism of the projective coordinate rings which is surjective on irrelevant ideals and is a variable-change homomorphism, respectively a coefficient homomorphism. The resulting rigid Weierstrass datum over $A_0$ — a curve with unit discriminant together with a $\Gamma_0$-power tuple of division-type polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point datum linked to it by `IsGamma1Link`, and a Drinfeld $\Gamma(q)$-basis pair of sections — is abbreviated $\mathcal{R}$ below.
--
--   **The fine moduli package and the Tate point.** $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` over $A_0$ for the moduli datum of $\mathcal{R}$: a ring $B_0$ with a universal point `P₀.univ` such that every point over every $A_0$-algebra is the image of the universal one under a unique $A_0$-algebra map. $x_0$ is a point of this datum over $K$ whose $j$-invariant has Laurent series `jqNModC k₀ q` (`hx₀`). $\mathrm{clC}$ is an $A_0$-algebra map from $B_0$ into the chart algebra `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` (the elements of $K$ integral over $A_0[j]$), and `hclC` says that it computes, after inclusion into $K$, the classifying map `P₀.classify x₀`. The hypothesis `hμ` pins $x_0$ at the Tate curve: it asserts the existence of a variable change $C$ over `LaurentSeries k₀` and a raw representative $r$ of $x_0$ such that (i) the coordinates of $C$ are normalised at the toric point `tateToricPoint k₀ q` of the $q$-th power of the root of unity attached to $\xi$, with $C.r = -1/12$, $C.s = -1/2$, $C.t = 1/24$ as constant series, (ii) the curve of $r$ becomes $C \cdot$ `tateBase k₀ q` over `LaurentSeries k₀`, (iii) for each prime factor $p$ of $M'$ and each primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta$ in a field receiving $k_0$, the corresponding $\Gamma_0$-power polynomial becomes the variable-change transform of the explicit product over $a \in [1, p^{v_p(M')}/2]$ prime to $p$ of $X$ minus the first coordinate of `toricPoint` at $\zeta^a$, (iv) the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-datum of $r$ becomes the variable-change transform of the `LevelPData` built from the toric point, and (v) the Drinfeld pair of $r$ passes through the four cusp coordinates of `cuspData k₀ q` at the $\ell_g$-th power of that root of unity, for the vectors $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$, after the variable change $C$. Lastly $\theta$ is a ring map from the residue field of $A_0$ to that of $A$ compatible with the residue maps (`hθ`).
--
--   **The conclusion.** Under these hypotheses, the following holds for every maximal ideal $y$ of the chart algebra `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` with residue $A_0$-algebra map $\psi$ onto the residue field of $A_0$ whose vanishing locus is exactly $y$; for every raw representative $x$ of $\mathcal{R}$ over the residue field $\kappa_0$ of $A_0$ whose class equals the image of the universal point under $\psi \circ \mathrm{clC}$; assuming the curve of $x$ has no non-trivial $q$-torsion over $\kappa_0$; for every point $g$ of that curve with `hg`: $g$ has exact order $M'$ and, for each prime factor $p$ of $M'$ and each multiple $n \cdot g$ that is an affine point $(x_1,y_1)$ of exact order $p^{v_p(M')}$, the $p$-th component of the $\Gamma_0$-power tuple of $x$ vanishes at $x_1$; and for every *admissible block* consisting of a field $\Omega$ over the residue field of $A$, a place $V$ of $\Omega$ over it, a ring map `res` from the residue field of $V$ back to the residue field of $A$ splitting the structure map, and a ring map $\rho$ from the chart algebra to the valuation ring of $V$, subject to: $\rho$ is compatible with $\theta$ on $A_0$-scalars, `res` composed with the residue of $V$ and $\rho$ equals $\theta \circ \psi$, the image $\rho(j)$ is transcendental over the residue field of $A$, and $\Omega$ is finite over the subfield generated by $\rho(j)$; further, an $A_0$-algebra structure on the valuation ring of $V$ with an $A_0$-algebra map $\rho_A$ from $B_0$ agreeing with $\rho \circ \mathrm{clC}$, a raw representative $x_V$ over that valuation ring of the image of the universal point under $\rho_A$, the condition $M' \neq 0$ in $\Omega$, a ring map $\chi_Q$ from the origin chart ring of the curve of $x_V$ presenting the section $Q$ of its Drinfeld pair after base change to $\Omega$ and sending `xOverY` and `zOverY` to $0$; and finally the level symmetry: $\gamma \in SL_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ and in $\Gamma_0(M')$ with both diagonal entries $\equiv 0 \bmod q$, an automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ over $k_0$ with `IsLevelAutAt k₀ q (ξ ^ ℓg) q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ` (so that $\tau$ acts on quotients of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$ by the slash action of the matrix attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, read through an embedding sending $\xi^{\ell_g}$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$), with $\tau$ preserving the chart algebra (`hpres`), and the hypothesis `hTR` that $\rho(h) = 0$ in $\Omega$ whenever all Laurent coefficients of $\tau(h)$ lie in the maximal ideal of $A_0$ — namely:
--
--   there exist a field $\Omega_1$ with an algebra structure over the residue field of $A$, a place $V_1$ of $\Omega_1$ over it, a splitting $\mathrm{res}_1$ of the residue field of $V_1$ onto the residue field of $A$, and a ring map $\rho_1$ from the chart algebra to the valuation ring of $V_1$, such that: $\mathrm{res}_1$ splits the structure map; $\rho_1$ is compatible with $\theta$ on $A_0$-scalars in $\Omega_1$; $\mathrm{res}_1$ composed with the residue of $V_1$ and $\rho_1$ equals $\theta \circ \psi$; $\rho_1(j)$ is transcendental over the residue field of $A$; and $\Omega_1$ is finite over the subfield generated by $\rho_1(j)$ — that is, $(\Omega_1, V_1, \mathrm{res}_1, \rho_1)$ is again an admissible block over $y$ — and moreover there exist a Weierstrass curve `curve` over the valuation ring of $V_1$ whose reduction has non-zero discriminant, a point `gen` of the curve over $\Omega_1$ of exact order $M'$, such that:
--
--   (1) the reduction `reduceHom` of `gen` again has exact order $M'$, and the $\Gamma_0(M')$-moduli point of the reduced curve with this reduced generator, transported by $\mathrm{res}_1$, coincides with the $\Gamma_0(M')$-moduli point of the pair (curve of $x$, $g$) transported by the $q$-power Frobenius of the residue field of $A$ composed with $\theta$, where the residue field of $A$ has characteristic $q$ by `charP_residueField_of_liesOverPrime_def`;
--
--   (2) for every algebraically closed field $\Omega'$ over $\Omega_1$, every non-zero $d \mid M'$, and every proof that the coefficient image of `qExpand ℚ d jq` lies in $K$ and in the chart algebra, the $j$-invariant `cyclicQuotientJ` at level $d$ of the quotient of the base change of `curve` to $\Omega'$ by the cyclic subgroup generated by $(M'/d)\cdot$`gen` equals the image in $\Omega'$ of $\rho_1$ evaluated at that chart element, i.e. at $j(\mathfrak q^d)$.
--
--   This is the step in the full-level argument at which, along the connected branch of the rigid $H_1$ chart over a place of the function field of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$, the admissible block is replaced by one over a finite extension carrying a good-reduction Weierstrass model whose $\Gamma_0(M')$-moduli point reduces to the $q$-power Frobenius twist of the given point, and whose cyclic quotients by the Drinfeld line realise the branch values $j(\mathfrak q^d)$. It is used in the derivation that the resulting place is a moduli place of the Frobenius-transformed point.
